<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fariburz I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fariburz I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a>, ruler of Shirvan in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, in the second half of the eleventh century. During his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> advanced into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and he accepted Seljuk overlordship and paid tribute to keep his throne. He belonged to the Persianised line of Shirvanshahs that modern historians call the Kasranids.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fariburz-i.jpg" alt="Fariburz I" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interfase · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirhams_of_Shirvanshah_I_Fariburz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Fakhr al-Din Fariburz ibn Sallar succeeded his father Sallar on 20 February 1063, having already governed most of the kingdom in his father's last years. Within weeks the Shaddadid ruler of Arran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-aswar-shavur-i">Abu'l-Aswar Shavur I</a>, invaded, probably because Fariburz sheltered a prince Shavur had deposed. After four invasions Fariburz made peace in 1064, paying 40,000 dinars for the return of the fortress of Quylamiyan.</p><p>From 1065 he fought the townspeople of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> (al-Bab), whose chiefs had killed his cousin, the emir Mansur ibn Abd al-Malik. He defeated them, executed many of their leaders and in 1066 installed his son Afridun as governor of the city. The struggle with Derbent's chiefs, who sought to govern the city themselves, continued through his reign.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> raided Shirvan from 1066, and the warlord Qara-tegin besieged the capital, Yazidiyya (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>), with Fariburz's uncle Mamlan ibn Yazid, a rival claimant. Fariburz paid 6,000 dinars to Savtegin, chamberlain of the Seljuq sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>, and Mamlan was killed on the night of 24 February 1067. Fariburz then undertook to pay a yearly tribute of 30,000 dinars for protection. When Alp Arslan entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> late in 1067, Fariburz submitted with gifts and in 1068 joined his campaign against Bagrat IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a>, Fariburz surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> to Savtegin, now holding it as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>', and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> paying an annual tax; the sources give the tribute variously as 70,000, later 40,000, or 100,000 dinars. His coins trace his standing: early issues give him the royal style al-malik al-adil, later ones of debased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> name Malik-Shah and give him no title, and the last, struck after 1094, name no sultan and call him al-Malik Fariburz again. No source records his death; coins and an inscription of 1096 naming his son Manuchihr II as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> place it between 1094 and 1096.</p><p>At its widest his realm reached from Mughan to Kumuk and Alania. The poet Masud ibn Namdar credits him with taking the Mughan steppe and with seizing Arran and appointing an official over Ganja, and he sought to subdue the Ghumiq of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, Baha al-Din Muhammad of the Kakuyid house, was with him a patron of men of letters. Sara Ashurbeyli judged that the Seljuqs, though they subjected Shirvan, could not end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a>'s power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fariburz I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Yazidids began as Arabs but became thoroughly Persianised: from the early eleventh century the rulers bore Persian names such as Manuchihr and <b>Fariburz</b> and claimed descent from the Sasanian kings, and modern historians often call this later line the Kasranids, after Kisra, the Arabic form of Khosrow.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.63,48.64&amp;year=1064" aria-label="Shamakhi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5743 712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Fariburz I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6193 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6193 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.63,48.64&amp;year=1064">What was here in 1064? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
